Dangling Pointer

Dangling pointer = a pointer which is no longer pointing to a valid memory location (the memory it pointed to has been freed/deallocated).

cint *ip;
{
    int a = 10;
    ip = &a;
    *ip = 35;     // valid here
}
// After block: a is destroyed (auto)
// ip still holds old address → dangling pointer
char *ip2 = (char *)ip;  // converting — still dangling
Memory analogy:
Hotel → room 'a' checked in → ip points to room 501
After a leaves (block ends) → room is freed
ip still points to 501 → dangling (memory not reserved by programmer)
To overcome this problem:

Make the local variable as static — then it persists.
After a block ends, if we try to access local variable address → dangling pointer issue.
